### 7. the review round, and the two branches nothing defended

Three lanes read the branch at 545a095f. Lane 1 (the arm) and lane 3 (the
documents) returned PASS. Lane 2 (the cases and the stub) returned FAIL on one
claim, which measurement confirmed.

FINDING 1 (lane 2) -- `if [ -z "$latest_rel" ]` is undefended.

  $ python3 -c "<delete the -z branch from release-check.sh>"
  removed
  $ cargo test --test falsification_release_check_latest_points_at_the_release
  running 4 tests
  test a_full_release_that_is_not_latest_is_named_and_red ... ok
  test a_pointer_that_cannot_be_read_is_unmeasured_and_red ... ok
  test a_prerelease_is_reported_and_not_forced_to_latest ... ok
  test a_full_release_that_is_latest_passes ... ok
  test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed

  The guard could be deleted and every case stayed green. Without it an empty
  answer falls to the comparison and the gate prints `still resolves to ` with a
  blank where the tag goes: the reader is told the pointer is wrong when what
  happened is that it never answered.

FINDING 2 (this session, no lane raised it) -- the refusal ran before the
question of what the release IS.

  `rc -ne 0` called fail() before `prerelease` was ever consulted. The arm's own
  comment says a prerelease is REPORTED, not refused. Whether that sentence is
  true depends on whether /releases/latest can fail to answer for a legitimate
  prerelease. MEASURED:

  $ gh api repos/electron/electron/releases?per_page=3 --jq '.[] | "\(.tag_name)\tpre=\(.prerelease)\tcreated=\(.created_at)"'
  v45.0.0-alpha.6	pre=true	created=2026-09-10T20:00:16Z
  v43.7.0	pre=false	created=2026-09-10T06:34:54Z
  v44.3.0	pre=false	created=2026-09-08T16:57:51Z
  $ gh api repos/electron/electron/releases/latest --jq '.tag_name'
  v44.3.0

  The newest release is a prerelease and /releases/latest answers a release two
  older: the endpoint serves only non-prerelease, non-draft releases. And when
  there is none to serve:

  $ gh api repos/paiml/pmat-empty-probe/releases/latest --jq '.tag_name'
  {"message":"Not Found", ... "status":"404"}gh: Not Found (HTTP 404)
  exit=1

  So for a repository whose releases are ALL prereleases the 404 is the ORDINARY
  state, and the shipped arm refused it as UNMEASURED -- forcing to latest
  exactly the deliberate prerelease it promised not to force. The sentence was
  true for forjar, which has full releases, and false for the instrument.

  Both silences now go through one `unread` reason and one branch on what the
  release is: reported for a prerelease, refused as UNMEASURED for a full one.

### 8. the kill matrix over the corrected arm

Six mutations, each applied to the COMMITTED script (190172ee) and restored with
`git checkout HEAD --`, every run `--no-fail-fast` so a failing binary does not
hide the next one. Both suites, ten cases, run every time.

  mutation                                             kills
  ---------------------------------------------------- ---------------------------------
  M1  prerelease+unread branch -> if false             a_prerelease_with_no_full_release_
                                                        behind_it_is_reported_not_refused
  M2  elif [ latest_rel != TAG ] -> elif false         a_full_release_that_is_not_latest_
                                                        is_named_and_red
  M3  elif [ latest_rel != TAG ] -> elif true          a_full_release_that_is_latest_passes
                                                        + 3 COLLATERAL (below)
  M4  elif [ -z latest_rel ] -> elif false             a_pointer_that_answers_with_nothing_
                                                        is_unmeasured_and_red
  M5  if [ rc -ne 0 ] -> if false                      a_pointer_that_cannot_be_read_is_
                                                        unmeasured_and_red
  M6  elif [ prerelease = true ] -> elif false         a_prerelease_is_reported_and_not_
                                                        forced_to_latest

  Five of the six kill exactly one case. M3 is the exception and it is disclosed
  here as it was in section 5: `elif true` refuses EVERY full release, so it also
  turns three pre-existing cases of falsification_release_check_says_what_is_pending
  red --  a_published_release_whose_docs_never_built_is_still_red,
  after_a_published_release_the_verdict_never_says_pre_tag and
  the_remaining_note_does_not_claim_a_present_file_is_missing. The fourth,
  before_the_tag_is_cut_the_verdict_still_says_so, survives because it is pre-tag
  and arm 2b never runs. M3 is a collateral kill, not a clean one, and it is the
  only case in the suite that is proven by an over-refusal rather than by removal.

  THE FIRST RUN OF THIS MATRIX WAS WRONG. It used `cargo test --test A --test B`
  without --no-fail-fast, cargo stopped after the first failing binary, and the
  says_what_is_pending suite never ran under any mutation. That run reported M3
  as a clean single kill. It is recorded here because the receipt would have
  carried that claim.

